      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional firm. Three answers: Allowed, Not allowed, Ask first. You'll sort a set of everyday 'can I?' moments against the firm's own rules, with the reason on screen, and handle a few messages that arrive in the inbox. When you're not sure, ask first.",
        "defendOnly": "Every one of these was an everyday call, the kind you make without thinking. Ask first is never the wrong answer, and an honest mistake reported quickly is fixed, not punished. Any you read the wrong way are picked out below, with the reason on screen. Not sure? Ask first. Made a mistake? Report it.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "Three answers, five responsibilities",
            "text": "Three answers cover almost everything: Allowed, Not allowed, and Ask first. No policy can list every situation, so when you're not sure, ask first, it's never the wrong answer. Five things are yours to do: protect your access, so passwords, codes, logins and badges are never shared; use approved tools, devices and storage; look at and share only the data your job needs; report anything suspicious, including your own mistakes; and ask first when you're unsure."
          },
          {
            "heading": "What's monitored, and the limits",
            "text": "Work systems are monitored to keep them safe, within rules: email and web filtering, sign-in records, and data leaving the firm. You must be told what is checked, why and how, and the law sets limits that protect you, so it isn't someone reading your messages for fun. If monitoring worries you, ask the DPO. Work messages are records too: write every one as if the person it's about could read it, because one day they might."
          }
        ],
        "mondayCard": "Pick the one person you'd ask when you're unsure: the service desk for software and devices, the security team for anything suspicious, the DPO for personal data, your manager for how your team works.",
        "noBlameLine": "Honest mistakes reported quickly are fixed, not punished. Deliberate misuse is different, and the policy says what happens. The difference is intent and openness.",
        "defendIntro": "Sort the everyday 'can I?' moments into Allowed, Not allowed or Ask first, with the reason on screen, then handle the short inbox. When you're not sure, Ask first, it's never the wrong answer. There's no clock on you here."
